    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "Done. The full test suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`), including the new `coreWarnsWhenApproachingCapacity` acceptance test.\n\n## What I changed\n\nThe warning follows the same persisted-flag pattern as the existing `bulkSignupWarning`, computed at creation time and read back on `GET`. I reused the count that `EnsureCityCapacity` already computes rather than adding a new pipeline step, keeping structural impact low.\n\n1. **`EnsureCityCapacity.java`** \u2014 one added line, `owner.setCapacityWarning(count >= WARN_THRESHOLD)` (threshold 40), placed after the `>= 50` rejection so a rejected owner never reaches it. The hard 409 at 50 is untouched. The loop already counts existing owners in the city, so no new query.\n2. **`Owner.java`** \u2014 new `capacityWarning` field with `@Column(name = \"capacity_warning\")` and a getter/setter beside `bulkSignupWarning`. MapStruct maps it into `OwnerDto` by name \u2014 no mapper change needed.\n3. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 `capacityWarning` read-only boolean property on the `Owner` schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` exposes it.\n4. **`db/hsqldb/schema.sql`** \u2014 added the `capacity_warning BOOLEAN` column (hsqldb is the profile the app and tests use; the postgres/mysql/h2 schema files are stale upstream copies the entity already doesn't match, so I left them alone).\n5. **`db/hsqldb/data.sql`** \u2014 the 10 seed owner rows use positional `INSERT`s, so each got one extra `NULL` in the new column's slot.\n\nBehavior: an accepted owner whose city already holds 40\u201349 owners now returns `capacityWarning: true`; below 40 it's `false`; at 50 the create is still rejected with the existing 409.",
